# Start a login console on the gadget's ACM serial port when it appears.
#
# The CDC-ACM function gives the host a /dev/ttyACM* that is already bound and
# idle — but nothing listens on our end, so it is dead weight. A getty there is
# a second, independent way into the device that needs no IP, no dongle and no
# UART adapter. During a reboot loop on 35071FDHS0017C there was no way in at
# all; this is that missing door.
#
# Started from udev rather than enabled via a .wants symlink ON PURPOSE.
# serial-getty@.service has BindsTo=/After=dev-%i.device, so an enabled
# serial-getty@ttyGS0 instance blocks boot ~90s waiting on dev-ttyGS0.device
# whenever the gadget isn't up (the default dongle/host-mode cable). That is why
# the mainline track disabled its instance outright. Triggering on the device
# node instead means it starts exactly when /dev/ttyGS0 exists and never delays
# a boot where it doesn't — the same condition-gating usb-gadget.service uses.
#
# BindsTo also means systemd stops the getty when the node goes away, so moving
# the cable back to the dongle cleans up after itself.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", KERNEL=="ttyGS0", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="serial-getty@ttyGS0.service"
